OpenConnect 9.21 is preserved verbatim in vendor/openconnect-9.21.tar.gz,
with its detached release signature and the signing public key. Its header
is copied verbatim to include/openconnect.h. Upstream copyright notices,
component licenses and complete source are retained inside the archive;
licenses/COPYING.LGPL is also extracted for distribution. OpenConnect is
LGPL-2.1-only. Combining our GPL-3.0-only bridge with the library makes the
combined work GPL-3.0-only without removing upstream notices or rights in
the unmodified upstream source. No code is copied from the reference GUI.

sources.json records URLs, SHA256 values and the official-page-published
signer fingerprint. The release signature was actually verified at input
acquisition; builds recheck digests and verify VALIDSIG using an isolated
GnuPG home. This is fingerprint-pinned verification, not a Web of Trust claim.

Invocation from workspace root:
  python3 native/build.py --target host --dependency-prefix /target/deps
The prefix flag is optional on Linux/macOS. Linux selects /opt/ocvpn-deps
when present, otherwise collects the Debian/Ubuntu /usr dependency closure
under target/native/<triple>/dependencies. Other Linux distributions can
provide an explicit prefix. macOS discovers brew --prefix, which must retain
the required license inventory. Windows requires an explicit MinGW prefix.
Explicit targets: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, x86_64-apple-darwin,
aarch64-apple-darwin, x86_64-pc-windows-msvc. Use each target's native runner.
Windows invokes Python from MSYS2 and builds the C ABI with MinGW-w64 GCC;
the consuming Rust application remains MSVC. Python >=3.10 needs the tarfile
security backport (data_filter). No host package installation is performed.

Linux container, with Docker context native/:
  docker build -f native/Dockerfile.linux -t ocvpn-native-linux native
  docker run --rm --user 1000:1000 --mount type=bind,src="$PWD",dst=/workspace ocvpn-native-linux
Only the repository is mounted. No host root mount, privileged flag or
network/administrative capability is needed. The image uses Ubuntu 22.04
native development packages; prepare-linux-prefix.py stages the recursive
ELF dependency closure and retains distro versions and license texts. The
image is a development recipe, not a source-locked release environment.

Artifacts:
  target/native/<resolved Rust triple>/source/openconnect-9.21/
  target/native/<resolved Rust triple>/stage/ocvpn-native/
The stage contains lib/, include/, share/licenses/ and native-manifest.json.
Its openconnect field is the upstream release (9.21); runtime_version is the
exact generated version.c identity (v9.21-unknown for this archive build).
The recipe rejects unexpected suffixes and removes inherited RPM version/
release and GIT_DIR overrides before generating the native version.
The manifest records actual dependency versions, bridge ABI, enforced HPKE
capability, and file digests. Repeated builds take an OS file lock and rebuild
only source/stage covered by the target's .ocvpn-build-owner.json marker.
Symlinked output roots and unowned or redirected source/stage are rejected.
Legacy outputs created before ownership markers must be relocated explicitly
once. The lock is released when the process exits; it is not a PID guess.
Package tooling
must install this stage in the application-owned trusted location. Never
load it from CWD or a path selected by a frontend. Library names and exports
follow upstream libtool, with ocgui_* functions in the same DLL/shared object.

The prefix must contain target-native shared dependencies and complete
share/licenses material. dependencies.json lists capability minima, not
source locks for all transitive libraries. Full source-locked dependency
releases are not delivered by this development recipe. Runtime closure is
checked and local loader paths are rewritten; Windows signed Wintun 0.14.1
is downloaded with SHA256 verification and its binary license retained.
macOS binaries require signing after install-name rewriting.

The build patches include the bridge and its ABI4 exports. The narrow GnuTLS
peer-policy patch exposes both successful and failed trust/hostname decisions
with callback-scoped certificate chains; absent a policy, upstream semantics
remain unchanged. Identical-certificate rehandshakes recheck policy and host.
Policy consumers must check exact host+port pins via the upstream hash API;
accepting one host never grants trust to a redirect host.
The HOTP patch treats failed counter commits as generation failures and wipes
the generated option before any submission. Consumers must abort on callback
failure rather than follow upstream's manual-token fallback.
The Array patch removes an unconditional upstream stdout debug print so the
library cannot corrupt its caller's structured CLI/IPC output.
Command handles are independently duplicated and non-inheritable; callers
serialize send/close while native context destruction may race sends. Unix
uses fd writes with thread-local SIGPIPE protection, Windows uses Winsock
duplication and send. See bridge/ocgui.h for ownership and error contracts.
The Unix script child retains its service-owned worker process group so
cancellation reaches network helpers. Protected inherited lifetime leases
also prevent journal recovery while orphaned mutator descendants remain.
Unix private worker IPC descriptors are close-on-exec; lifetime descriptors
deliberately survive native helper exec.
Private TUN fields are configured in C using matching native strdup/free;
mainloop still decides when to create TUN. The resolver bridge calls native
getaddrinfo/freeaddrinfo; Rust must not construct or free addrinfo lists.
The fixed progress sink is borrowed, synchronous, bounded and unredacted;
consumers must redact/filter before diagnostics.

The GP prelogin browser negotiation patch adapts the protocol value mappings,
parameter placement and saml-default-browser advertisement semantics from
yuezk/GlobalProtect-openconnect (GPL-3.0), pinned commit
9e4f45997ebee452c58d7d3066dcfc175d65ef82:
https://github.com/yuezk/GlobalProtect-openconnect/tree/9e4f45997ebee452c58d7d3066dcfc175d65ef82
Reference paths: crates/gpapi/src/params/portal_prelogin.rs,
crates/gpapi/src/params/gateway_prelogin.rs,
crates/gpapi/src/os_profile/mod.rs and crates/gpapi/src/portal/prelogin.rs.
This does not copy or depend on its proprietary GUI wrapper. The patch keeps
the pinned native HTTP/TLS and portal/gateway authentication implementation.
An explicit negative browser advertisement permits one requested embedded
prelogin retry per phase, never after authentication rejection. Missing or
malformed advertisements remain unknown. Reported Linux, Mac and Windows
identities determine negotiation. Other reported platforms preserve upstream
prelogin without inventing a browser value; system-browser SSO is unsupported.
The configured preference is retained independently for gateway prelogin.
The GP SAML field patch preserves the detector's actual cookie field name
instead of submitting browser tokens as passwd. Independently requested
gateway SAML has a fresh account field and cannot silently reuse the portal's
ordinary-password retry path. Explicit portal-cookie reuse remains intact.
The command-descriptor initialization patch prevents freeing a context without
a command pipe from closing the caller's descriptor zero.

Offline browser certificate verification uses the same native GnuTLS runtime,
DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo hash construction as gnutls.c, and upstream pin
comparison on an owned temporary OpenConnect context. A supplied pin is the
only authority; without one, system/explicit PEM CA roots, chain validity,
TLS-server key purpose/usage and hostname are checked. The verifier does not
fetch AIA/OCSP data, so offline verification is not online revocation proof.
Certificate buffers are borrowed, bounded and never logged. See ocgui.h for
exact output sizes, ownership, return codes and caller host-scoping duties.

Native verification entrypoint:
  cargo xtask verify protocols
It builds development-only Rust drivers and installs pinned Python fixture
dependencies into target/fixture-venv. The signed upstream TLS fixtures run
on loopback with fresh private test certificates; no host trust changes.
Linux execution has verified real authentication for Cisco multicert,
Juniper, Pulse, GP, F5 and Fortinet, including separate GP MFA and portal-cookie
cases. Array coverage is protocol selection and TLS rejection, not successful
authentication. Strict redirect pinning, command-handle lifetime and failed
HOTP commits are exercised against the actual library. The HOTP fixture
observes endpoint counts only and verifies no uncommitted OTP was submitted.
The Cisco fixture requires pyOpenSSL 25.3.0: version 26 removed its upstream
X509.get_extension dependency. Browser SSO, tunneling, native cross-platform
acceptance and vendor appliance interoperability are separate obligations.

Linux native WebKit SSO has additionally been exercised against the signed
upstream GP fixture with independent portal/gateway SAML, required JavaScript
and comments-only completion. Both identical and different confirmed accounts
were checked; observed submissions used prelogin-cookie, never passwd, and
the final native handoff matched the confirmed gateway account. This is
authentication proof, not tunnel or vendor-appliance interoperability.
The offline certificate-policy driver checks preservation of caller-owned
stdin as well as custom CA, hostname and strict host/port pin decisions.

Unix network lifecycle fork:
packaging/common/vpnc-script retains the GPL-2.0-or-later copyright/license
notice and the executed do_connect/do_disconnect orchestration from
vpnc-scripts ce9e961bd0f6b867e1c7c35f78f6fb973f6ff101. Its route, interface
and resolver mutation functions now use an acknowledged private pipe protocol
to the ocvpn-net journal owner, not an arbitrary executable or shell command.
Connect and reconnect invoke do_connect; disconnect invokes do_disconnect
before reverse-order conditional journal recovery. An unknown, repeated or
omitted operation is an error. Each operation's intent is durable before
mutation and its observed result is durable before shell acknowledgement.
An interrupted unobserved operation remains explicit recovery state.

Reviewed differences: only Linux and Darwin are supported by this Unix fork.
Linux retains nss-resolve/managed-resolv.conf detection and preference for
systemd-resolved, then resolvconf. Darwin uses utun dynamic-store DNS and
IPv4/IPv6 service keys. Backend changes after preflight abort connect.
Disconnect uses the journaled backend rather than today's resolver preference.
DNS writes/restores are per-link/per-service/per-field; physical-service
networksetup preferences, global resolv.conf backups and unconditional scutil
key removal are deliberately not used. Split domains use resolved routing
domains or Darwin SupplementalMatchDomains. resolvconf cannot represent split
DNS and returns an explicit error rather than leaking those queries.

The validated route planner normalizes IPv4/IPv6 includes/excludes and
connected-prefix overrides before passing route lists to the upstream loops.
Full tunnels use explicit paired /1 routes without replacing physical /0s.
Physical transport and exclusion bypasses precede interface configuration.
Kernel-generated address routes and Linux address-generation mode are in the
preflight inventory. Device creation, module loading, discovery of hooks,
configuration sourcing, backup-file writes and unrelated OS branches were
removed. The native owner preserves the boot/interface identity and restores
only values still matching the transaction's observed/intended ownership.
The installed script is opened without following symlinks, ownership-checked,
byte-checked against the embedded asset, and executed by its open descriptor.
All subprocesses remain within the supervised worker lifetime and deadlines.
Native Linux/Darwin tunnel, resolver, reconnect and crash-recovery acceptance
must be exercised on isolated native runners; this notice asserts no OS run.
